                    round_name_r4:          `Oversight & Defend`,

                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the failed contract`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `The incumbent supplying oxygen and home-care to <strong>1,900 dependent patients</strong> has defaulted, and the surge hits in <strong>63 days</strong>. Resist the reflex to blame bad luck or careless staff. The original award went to the lowest bidder with <em>no SLAs, no monitoring and no exit triggers</em> — a design failure that let quality erode to 84% on-time until the vendor collapsed. Diagnose <em>why</em> it failed, then choose the Lei 14.133/2021 instrument that fits the clock. A traditional pregão takes 75–90 days; <strong>técnica e preço</strong> is a fast competitive route that weights quality. Falling back to dispensa emergencial is the failure mode, not the safety net.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Select the vendor on a published, defensible model`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Three viable bids: the <strong>lowest at R$18,6M</strong> with a weak ~84% track record, a <strong>mid bid at R$21,2M</strong> (~95% and willing to commit to SLAs), and a <strong>premium at R$24,3M</strong> bundling 24/7 SLAs and telemetry. Under the transparency mandate your weighting and criteria are <em>public and auditable</em>. The cheapest saves money on paper but its 84%-equivalent record endangers patients and invites a re-default; each point below 98% maps to <em>~19 at-risk patients</em>. Set a price-vs-quality weighting you could reproduce in front of the TCE, set a published minimum threshold, and justify any non-lowest-price choice.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Design the contract incentives`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Structure the incentive architecture inside the <strong>±8% band (≈R$1,76M either way)</strong>. Word the on-time-delivery SLA so it is an <em>exact, verifiable metric</em> — 'best efforts' is unenforceable and gives you leverage on paper and none in practice. Calibrate the bonus and penalty: incentives too weak don't move behaviour, while a maxed-out penalty with no bonus invites price-padding, metric-gaming, and good bidders simply not bidding next time. Then write a measurable <strong>exit / termination trigger</strong> with a pre-qualified backup, so the city is never again trapped with a failing vendor.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Choose oversight, absorb the shock & defend`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Pick how the contract is monitored. In-house monitoring with 22 stretched staff is how the last contract eroded unseen; telemetry costs <strong>~R$240k/year</strong>, while a default in a surge costs <em>~R$140k a week</em> plus a TCE injunction. Then a shock lands: week-1 delivery dips to 95% and a losing bidder files a challenge. Show your oversight catches it early and that your published matrix answers the challenge. Finally, defend to a mock TCE / Controladoria panel that the award is <strong>fast, cheaper over its life, AND accountable</strong> — agility and accountability are complementary, not opposed.`,

                    r1_root_label:          `Why did the original R$22M contract actually fail?`,
                    r1_instrument_label:    `Which Lei 14.133/2021 instrument fits the 63-day surge clock?`,
                    r1_frame_label:         `How clearly does your root-cause memo frame the agility-vs-accountability trade-off? (%)`,
                    r1_frame_readout:       `Clarity of the decision frame:`,
                    root_design:            `A design failure — lowest-price-only award, no SLAs, no monitoring, no exit triggers`,
                    root_vendor:            `A bad-luck vendor — its supplier line collapsed, nothing we could have foreseen`,
                    root_price:             `We simply paid too little — the budget was unrealistic from the start`,
                    root_staff:             `The contract managers were careless and stopped checking deliveries`,
                    inst_tecnica:           `Técnica e preço — a fast competitive award weighting quality and price together`,
                    inst_pregao:            `Pregão (lowest price) — the traditional 75–90 day route, simplest to defend`,
                    inst_dialogo:           `Diálogo competitivo — rich dialogue, but too slow for a 63-day clock`,
                    inst_dispensa:          `Dispensa emergencial now — skip competition, sign a single vendor today`,

                    r2_weight_label:        `Set your published evaluation weighting (price vs. technical/quality — must total 100%)`,
                    r2_weight_readout:      `Weighting total:`,
                    r2_weight_pct:          `% / 100%`,
                    r2_vendor_label:        `Which of the three bids do you recommend awarding?`,
                    r2_minthreshold_label:  `Set a published minimum on-time-delivery threshold any bidder must clear (%)`,
                    r2_minthreshold_hint:   `Clinical guidance requires ≥98% for oxygen-dependent patients; each point below 98% maps to ~19 at-risk patients.`,
                    w_price:                `Headline price weight`,
                    w_quality:              `Technical / quality & track-record weight`,
                    vendor_balanced:        `Mid bid — R$21,2M, on-time track record ~95%, will commit to SLAs (best value)`,
                    vendor_cheap:           `Lowest bid — R$18,6M, weak ~84% track record, no service guarantees`,
                    vendor_premium:         `Premium bid — R$24,3M, 24/7 SLAs + telemetry, on-time ~99%`,

                    r3_sla_label:           `How do you word the on-time-delivery SLA?`,
                    r3_penalty_label:       `Where do you set the penalty on an SLA miss, within the ±8% band? (% of contract value)`,
                    r3_penalty_readout:     `Penalty on a sustained SLA miss:`,
