                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_root_right:    `<strong>That's the right diagnosis.</strong> The contract didn't fail by bad luck — it was a design failure. A lowest-price-only award with no SLAs, no monitoring and no exit triggers let quality erode to 84% until the vendor collapsed. Fix the design and you won't recreate the default.`,
                    coach_r1_root_vendor:   `<strong>'Bad luck' is the comfortable answer.</strong> The vendor's collapse was foreseeable: you awarded on price alone with no service-level incentives and weak monitoring, so you never saw quality eroding. That is a design failure, not an act of God.`,
                    coach_r1_root_price:    `<strong>Underpricing wasn't the root cause.</strong> Three viable bids exist within budget — the problem was awarding on headline price with no SLAs or monitoring, not the size of the number. Diagnose the design, not the budget.`,
                    coach_r1_root_staff:    `<strong>Blaming the contract managers misses the system.</strong> With no measurable SLAs and no monitoring, even diligent staff had nothing to enforce. The design gave them no leverage — that's what failed.`,
                    coach_r1_instrument_sharp: `<strong>Sharp framing.</strong> You named it a design failure AND chose técnica e preço — a competitive instrument that weights quality and still fits inside the 63-day clock. That is treating instrument choice as a value lever, not a legal formality.`,
                    coach_r1_pregao:        `<strong>A lowest-price pregão recreates the trap — and blows the clock.</strong> At 75–90 days you miss the day-49 award and the day-63 surge, and you've reopened the exact lowest-price-only route that just defaulted. There was a faster competitive instrument you skipped.`,
                    coach_r1_dialogo:       `<strong>Diálogo competitivo is too slow here.</strong> The dialogue is rich, but it cannot land an award inside 63 days. You'll miss the surge and be forced into emergency dispensa anyway — the worst of both.`,
                    coach_r1_dispensa:      `<strong>Dispensa emergencial on day one throws away the mandate.</strong> No competition, single vendor, signed under pressure — it's legal and fast, and it's a banner headline confirming procurement reform failed. Agility is the point of reform, not a reason to abandon competition.`,
                    coach_r1_vagueframe:    `<strong>A vague memo won't survive the TCE.</strong> If your decision frame doesn't clearly name the agility-vs-accountability trade-off, the auditors and the losing bidders will write that story for you. Make the reasoning reproducible.`,
                    coach_r1_slowclock:     `<strong>Watch the clock.</strong> Your instrument is competitive but you're drifting past the day-49 award window — every week of delay adds ~R$140k of avoidable UPA admissions. Tighten the timeline.`,
                    coach_r1_surgemiss:     `<strong>You've missed the day-63 surge.</strong> The instrument you picked simply can't award in time, so the city is now forced into emergency dispensa with patients already overflowing the UPAs. That's the failure you were tasked to prevent.`,
                    coach_r2_weight_balanced: `<strong>A defensible best-value model.</strong> A quality-leaning but balanced weighting prices in technical capability and track record without ignoring cost — and crucially it's a model you can reproduce in front of the TCE and the losing bidders.`,
                    coach_r2_weight_priceheavy: `<strong>That's the lowest-price reflex in weighting form.</strong> Loading price weight externalises quality and continuity risk — exactly how the last contract defaulted. Best-value frameworks weight track record and life-cycle cost, not just the headline.`,
                    coach_r2_weight_qualityonly: `<strong>Quality-only is also indefensible.</strong> Ignore price entirely and you can't justify the spend to the TCE either — 'we just bought the best' is as unreproducible as 'we just bought the cheapest'. Weight both, transparently.`,
                    coach_r2_vendor_balanced: `<strong>Best value, not lowest price.</strong> The R$21,2M mid bid prices in a ~95% track record and willingness to commit to SLAs. It costs more than the cheapest on paper but de-risks the surge and is defensible against a TCE review.`,
                    coach_r2_vendor_cheap:  `<strong>You just recreated the contract that defaulted.</strong> R$18,6M looks great in the audit, but an ~84% track record against a 98% clinical requirement means ~266 at-risk patients — and a vendor likely to slip in week 5 of a surge. What does lowest price actually cost you?`,
                    coach_r2_vendor_premium_ok: `<strong>The premium bid is defensible here.</strong> R$24,3M de-risks the surge with 24/7 SLAs and telemetry, and because your published weighting actually values quality, you can justify the spend to the TCE. Watch the life-cycle cost ceiling.`,
                    coach_r2_vendor_premium_unjust: `<strong>You picked the dearest bid 'because it's better'.</strong> Under the transparency mandate that sentence is now a public document — and your price-heavy weighting doesn't support it. An auditor and a losing bidder will read it. Show the matrix that justifies the spend.`,
                    coach_r2_lowthreshold:  `<strong>Your minimum threshold is too low.</strong> Setting the bar well under the 98% clinical requirement means even the floor you publish endangers oxygen-dependent patients — each point below 98% is ~19 at-risk people. Raise it.`,
                    coach_r3_sla_measurable: `<strong>That SLA is enforceable.</strong> An exact ≥98% metric verified weekly via telemetry, with a defined cure period and monthly audit, gives you real leverage — not 'incentives on paper and zero leverage in practice'.`,
                    coach_r3_sla_reasonable: `<strong>'Best efforts' is unenforceable.</strong> In month 3 the vendor is at 92% and your penalty clause is dead because 'reasonable efforts' isn't measurable. What's the exact metric, threshold, and the exact range of bonus/penalty within your band?`,
                    coach_r3_sla_annual:    `<strong>An annual average hides the misses.</strong> By the time the year-end review shows a problem, oxygen-dependent patients have already gone without. Measure weekly, with a cure period, or the SLA can't protect anyone.`,
                    coach_r3_calibrated:    `<strong>That's the calibration point.</strong> A moderate penalty that bites plus a fair bonus that rewards moves behaviour without inviting gaming — and with telemetry to verify, the vendor can't reclassify late deliveries. Incentives that move behaviour, not waste.`,
                    coach_r3_overload:      `<strong>You stacked the full penalty with no reward.</strong> Watch what the vendor does: pad the price to absorb the risk, game the metric by reclassifying late deliveries, or good bidders simply don't bid next time. Aggressive penalties without a fair bonus just shift risk back into your price.`,
                    coach_r3_toothless:     `<strong>Those incentives don't move behaviour.</strong> A near-zero penalty and near-zero bonus is the absent-incentive drift that let the last contract erode. The ±8% band exists to be used — calibrate it.`,
                    coach_r3_nobonus:       `<strong>All stick, no carrot.</strong> A real penalty with no bonus to reward over-performance reads as one-sided and pushes the vendor to do the minimum and pad the price. Pair the penalty with a fair bonus.`,
                    coach_r3_discretion:    `<strong>A discretionary exit clause is weak leverage.</strong> 'Termination for cause at the city's discretion' is exactly what gets litigated when you try to use it. Define the breach thresholds and line up a backup vendor in advance.`,
                    coach_r3_noexit:        `<strong>No exit trigger is how you get trapped again.</strong> Without defined breach thresholds, step-in rights and a pre-qualified backup, a failing vendor holds 1,900 patients hostage — exactly the situation you're recovering from now.`,
                    coach_r4_hybrid:        `<strong>Oversight as value-protecting infrastructure.</strong> Telemetry plus in-house management plus periodic third-party audit catches problems early, gives you live evidence, and carries audit credibility — for ~R$240k against a R$140k-a-week default.`,
                    coach_r4_inhouse:       `<strong>In-house only is how the last contract eroded unseen.</strong> Your 22 stretched staff didn't catch the decline until the vendor collapsed. Oversight is the price of moving fast safely — what is the 'no extra cost' option really costing you?`,
                    coach_r4_telemetryonly: `<strong>Telemetry with nobody acting on it is half a system.</strong> A dashboard catches the dip, but without in-house management to trigger the cure period and an audit for credibility, the data just scrolls past. Pair the live data with governance.`,
                    coach_r4_thirdpartyonly: `<strong>A third-party audit alone finds problems late.</strong> Periodic audit lends credibility but no live data — by the time it reports a dip, the surge has already hit oxygen-dependent patients. You need real-time telemetry alongside it.`,
                    coach_r4_shock_evidence: `<strong>That's oversight earning its keep.</strong> Triggering the cure period on telemetry evidence catches the 95% dip early, and publishing the scored matrix answers the bidder challenge with a reproducible record. Fast and accountable at once.`,
                    coach_r4_shock_ignore:  `<strong>Ignoring the dip is how the last contract died.</strong> 'Probably noise' at 95% in week one, unhandled, is precisely the quiet erosion that ended in default. And a challenge handled 'quietly' instead of with the published matrix invites a TCE injunction.`,
                    coach_r4_shock_dispensa: `<strong>Tearing up the award for dispensa loses the mandate.</strong> A 95% dip in week one is what your SLA and cure period are FOR — not a reason to abandon a lawful competitive award for an emergency no-bid one. You'd solve nothing and confirm the reform failed.`,
                    coach_r4_def_value:     `<strong>That's a TCE-ready case.</strong> Faster than a pregão, cheaper over the life of the service once default and surge costs are priced in, and accountable through a published matrix, enforceable SLA math and an oversight log. That is public value, demonstrated.`,
                    coach_r4_def_cheapest:  `<strong>'We awarded the lowest price' is not the safe line you think.</strong> It's the line that recreated this crisis. Defending on headline price alone tells the panel you learned nothing about life-cycle cost and continuity risk.`,
                    coach_r4_def_trust:     `<strong>'Trust us, it's better quality' is how officers end up explaining themselves to the TCE.</strong> Under the transparency mandate, a choice without a reproducible scoring model anyone can read is indefensible. Show the matrix or don't make the claim.`,

