                    insight_value:          `Public value created: a fast competitive award, cheaper over its life once default and surge costs are priced in, and accountable through a published matrix, enforceable SLAs and live oversight. You proved agility and accountability are complementary.`,
                    insight_indefensible:   `Fast but indefensible: you moved quickly, but without reproducible criteria, enforceable SLAs or credible oversight the award would not survive a TCE review — and accountability is half of public value.`,
                    insight_risky:          `Cheap but risky: the headline cost looks good, but a weak track record and thin SLAs leave 1,900 oxygen-dependent patients exposed. This is how the last contract defaulted — life-cycle cost, not headline price, is the real number.`,
                    insight_short:          `You fell short of demonstrable public value. Re-examine where you reverted to lowest-price reflexes, vague incentives, or under-invested oversight — each one quietly drained agility, quality or accountability.`,
                    insight_dispensa:       `You fell back on dispensa emergencial — a single-vendor, no-competition award signed under pressure. It solved the surge and lost the mandate: a banner headline confirming procurement reform failed. There was a faster competitive route.`,
                    insight_surge:          `You blew past the day-63 surge clock. Every week of delay added ~R$140k of avoidable UPA admissions and forced an emergency award with patients already overflowing — agility is the point of the reform.`,
                    insight_lowquality:     `Service quality stayed low: a weak track record and thin SLAs mean oxygen-dependent patients are exposed. Best-value procurement weights quality and writes enforceable SLAs, not just a low headline price.`,
